## 4. Frontend Architecture (Vanilla JavaScript)

The frontend is designed as a Single Page Application (SPA) using modular, self-invoking functions (IIFE pattern) to ensure encapsulation and avoid global namespace pollution.

### 4.1 Core Modules (`js/core/`)
*   `api_client.js`: Centralized HTTP client using the Fetch API. Features automatic retries (up to 2 times) with exponential backoff, request timeouts (60s), and abort controllers.
*   `app.js`: Manages global application state (selected transformer, active year/month, active forecasting method) and handles routing/navigation.
*   `data.js`: Manages local data caching, handles local storage for custom transformers, and coordinates monthly data loading.
*   `theme.js`: Manages dark/light theme switching and persists user preference in `localStorage`.

### 4.2 Business Logic & Calculations (`js/modules/`)
*   `calculations.js`: Replicates backend risk assessment logic on the client side. Computes cumulative monthly summaries, active/reactive ratios, and formats numbers according to Turkish locale standards (`tr-TR`).

### 4.3 UI Components (`js/ui/`)
*   `scada_sld_v4.js`: Renders and manages the interactive Single Line Diagram (SLD). Establishes a WebSocket connection to receive live telemetry, handles breaker toggles, and displays SCADA alarms.
*   `charts.js`: Configures global Chart.js defaults (fonts, colors, tooltips) and manages responsive rendering of load profiles, power triangles, and forecast comparisons.
*   `dashboard.js`: Renders the main overview, including summary cards, active alerts, and the forecast banner.
*   `forecast_ui.js`: Handles the forecasting interface, allowing users to run scenario simulations (e.g., adding a virtual solar plant or industrial load) and compare different ML models.
*   `maneuver_ui.js`: Displays AI-suggested maneuvers, allows operators to simulate topology changes, and displays before/after risk comparisons.
*   `topology.js`: Manages the interactive power triangle visualization and handles bulk topology updates.

